epmind/formal-conjectures#7613 · Filter by author JarekDuda opened on Oct 25, 2026\n\n  * ### Proposed contribution: graph bootstrap percolation conjectures and supporting API\n\ngoogle-deepmind/formal-conjectures#7597 · Filter by author Raj-Parekh24 opened on Oct 25, 2026\n\n  * ### Value-question answers that ignore a quantified parameter: Erdos 142, 319, 321, 361, 409 (sigma), 503, 692 (parts.ii)\n\ngoogle-deepmind/formal-conjectures#7590 · Filter by author KilianTheMagnificent opened on Oct 24, 2026\n\n  * ### arXiv:2501.03234 (ArithmeticSumS): verified reduction of all four open conjectures to bounds on a single quantity rest(k), plus exact row identities\n\ngoogle-deepmind/formal-conjectures#7589 · Filter by author Stayfoool opened on Oct 24, 2026\n\nPrevious1234567…32Next--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA logarithmic independence bound for clique-free graphs (https://github.com/openai/math/tree/main/preprints/A-Logarithmic-Independence-Bound-for-Clique-Free-Graphs-September-25-2026)\nciteturn2academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Authors: OpenAI ... For every fixed integer r ≥ 4, every K_{r}-free graph on n vertices with average degree d ≥ 2 has an independent set of size at least $c_r n\\log d/d$, where $c_r\\gt 0$ depends only on r. This proves the fixed-clique-size independence conjecture of Ajtai, Erdős, Komlós and Szemerédi.\nTitle: A logarithmic independence bound for clique-free graphs\nAuthors: OpenAI\nDate: Fri Sep 25 00:00:00 2026\n\nFor every fixed integer r ≥ 4, every K_{r}-free graph on n vertices with average degree d ≥ 2 has an independent set of size at least $c_r n\\log d/d$, where $c_r\\gt 0$ depends only on r. This proves the fixed-clique-size independence conjecture of Ajtai, Erdős, Komlós and Szemerédi.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nformal-conjectures/site/README.md at main · google-deepmind/formal-conjectures · GitHub (https://github.com/google-deepmind/formal-conjectures/blob/main/site/README.md)\nciteturn2search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; If `GITHUB_TOKEN` or `GH_TOKEN` is available, the build enriches matching contributors with their GitHub username, profile URL, and avatar URL; otherwise it falls back to the display names available in git history. ... `/about/`  | About the project ...     lake build FormalConjectures:literate FormalConjecturesForMathlib:literate FormalConjecturesUtil:literate ... Your site will be live at `https://<username>.github.io/formal-conjectures/` once the Action completes (typically ~2 minutes for a website-only build).\n\n# (work-in-progress) formal-conjectures-website\n\nTHIS IS EXPERIMENTAL FOR NOW\n\nFeatures to add:\n\n  * form to allow contribution suggestions\n  * conjecture upvoting system?\n\nWebsite for the Formal Conjectures Lean 4 repository.\n\n## Architecture\n\nThe site is a static website generated from a JSON metadata file produced by the formal-conjectures repository. The overall data flow is:\n    \n    `formal-conjectures repo (Lean 4)\n      └─ lake exe extract_names\n           └─ data/conjectures.json   ← added here by formal-conjectures CI\n                └─ node build.js\n                     └─ site/         ← deployed to GitHub Pages\n    `\n\n  1. Data extraction. The formal-conjectures CI runs `lake exe extract_names` (defined in `scripts/extract_names.lean`). This imports all compiled Lean modules, reads the `@[category]` and `@[AMS]` attributes, and writes a JSON array to stdout. Each entry contains the theorem's fully-qualified name, module, category, AMS subject codes, pretty-printed statement, and docstring.\n    \n    # Create a webtest branch from main\n    git checkout -b my-feature-webtest origin/main\n    \n    # Make your website changes (CSS, JS, templates, etc.)\n    # ...\n    \n    # Push to your fork — CI builds and deploys automatically\n    git push <your-fork> my-feature-webtest\n\nYour site will be live at `https://<username>.github.io/formal-conjectures/` once the Action completes (typically ~2 minutes for a website-only build).\n\n> Note: The naming convention `*-webtest` is what triggers the fast website-only build. Branches without this suffix run the full Lean build.\n\n> Note: GitHub Pages serves one deployment at a time per repo. Deploying from a webtest branch replaces what was deployed from `main`. This is fine for a fork used for previewing.\n\n## CI / deployment\n\nThe GitHub Actions workflow in `.github/workflows/build-and-docs.yml` triggers on pushes to `main` and `*-webtest` branches, as well as on pull requests.\n\nBuild modes:\n\nCondition  | Build mode  | What happens\n--- | --- | ---\nPush to `main` on upstream  | Full build  | Lean compilation + docs + website\nPush to `*-webtest` branch  | Website-only  | Downloads live data, builds website only (~2 min)\nManual `workflow_dispatch` with `website_only=true`  | Website-only  | Same as above\nPull request  | Full build  | Lean compilation + docs + website (no deploy)\n\nDeployment happens on pushes to `main`, and on `*-webtest` branches when on a fork.\n\nFor GitHub Pages setup (including the environment rule needed for `*-webtest` branches), see One-time fork setup above.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nformal-conjectures/PROOFS.md at main · google-deepmind/formal-conjectures · GitHub (https://github.com/google-deepmind/formal-conjectures/blob/main/PROOFS.md)\nciteturn2search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Prove a `test` or `API` declaration when the proof is short and useful. ... Every theorem in `FormalConjecturesForMathlib/` must have a complete kernel-checked proof. ... Run `#print axioms` on the linked theorem when the project is available.\n\n# Proof contributions\n\nFormal Conjectures is mainly a statement repository. Keep proofs short. For a long proof, prefer an external proof and record it with the `formal_proof` attribute.\n\nDo not add an informal proof of an open research problem.\n\n## Tests and API declarations\n\nProve a `test` or `API` declaration when the proof is short and useful. A long proof may remain `by sorry` in `FormalConjectures/`. Do not add a large proof only to remove that placeholder.\n\nFor a large computation in a `category test` declaration, `native_decide` may be acceptable. Review this case by case. Do not use it in `FormalConjecturesForMathlib/`.\n\n## FormalConjecturesForMathlib\n\nEvery theorem in `FormalConjecturesForMathlib/` must have a complete kernel-checked proof. Do not use `sorry`, placeholder definitions, or `native_decide` anywhere in that directory. Keep the code at Mathlib quality and import only the Mathlib modules that it needs.\n\n## Check proof claims\n\nA file that contains `sorry` can still compile. Before you claim that a theorem is formally proved, inspect its axioms:\n    \n    #print axioms my_theorem\n    -- [propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]\n\n`sorryAx` means that the theorem is not proved. Native evaluation can add axioms such as `Lean.ofReduceBool` or `Lean.trustCompiler`; report them when they are relevant.\n\nFor a `formal_proof` link:\n\n  1. Open the exact file and declaration that the link cites.\n  2. Check that the linked statement implies the repository statement.\n  3. Check for `sorry` and custom axioms.\n  4. Run `#print axioms` on the linked theorem when the project is available.\n  5. Use the correct `formal_proof` kind and a stable link.\n\nA repository name, a successful build, or a theorem with a similar name is not enough evidence.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA linear list-coloring bound in terms of the Hadwiger number (https://github.com/openai/math/tree/main/preprints/A-linear-list-coloring-bound-in-terms-of-the-Hadwiger-number-September-23-2026)\nciteturn2academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Authors: OpenAI ... We prove that every finite nonempty graph G satisfies $\\chi_{\\mathrm{list}}(G)\\le C h(G)$ for an absolute integer C, where $h(G)$ is the largest order of a clique minor.\nTitle: A linear list-coloring bound in terms of the Hadwiger number\nAuthors: OpenAI\nDate: Wed Sep 23 00:00:00 2026\n\nWe prove that every finite nonempty graph G satisfies $\\chi_{\\mathrm{list}}(G)\\le C h(G)$ for an absolute integer C, where $h(G)$ is the largest order of a clique minor. This resolves the Linear List Hadwiger conjecture affirmatively.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPull requests · google-deepmind/formal-conjectures · GitHub (https://github.com/google-deepmind/formal-conjectures/pulls)\nciteturn2search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today;   1. google-deepmind ...   * ### feat(Arxiv/math.0608009): formal proof links for the Dixmier conjecture ...   * ### feat(WrittenOnTheWallII): add Conjecture 372 and related variants\n\n  * ### fix(GreensOpenProblems/29): separate the open problem from the false subset variant\n\ngreen-problems\n\n#7810·Qunsheng-Zhang opened Nov 5, 2026·\n\n·\n\n1\n\n  * ### fix(ErdosProblems/143): `erdos_143.parts.i` is true\n\nerdos-problems\n\n#7807·ashm1tr opened Nov 4, 2026 Contributor·\n\n·\n\n1\n\n  * ### feat(ErdosProblems/1129, ErdosProblems/1130): add Erdős Problems 1129 and 1130\n\nawaiting-author erdos-problems\n\n#7801·veljjanoski opened Nov 4, 2026 Contributor·\n\n·\n\n2\n\n  * ### Add Hirzebruch's question on line arrangements (arXiv:1607.07709)\n\narxiv\n\n#7794·MdeBorbon opened Nov 3, 2026·\n\n·\n\n1\n\n  * ### feat(WrittenOnTheWallII): add Conjecture 372 and related variants\n\n  * ### feat(ErdosProblems/835): link formal proof of indepNum_johnson_le_johnsonBound\n\nerdos-problems\n\n#7790·karlesmarin opened Nov 3, 2026·\n\n·\n\n  * ### feat(ErdosProblems/963): link Lean proof of the greedy variant\n\nerdos-problems\n\n#7789·karlesmarin opened Nov 3, 2026·\n\n·\n\n1\n\n  * ### feat(ErdosProblems/338): disprove Kelly'